# Euler's identity, as half a turn
subtitle: "Why e to the i pi equals minus one: multiplying by i is a quarter turn, so e to the i pi is half a turn around the unit circle."

## Euler's identity, as half a turn
domain: {"x":[-1.55,2.45],"y":[-1.37,1.37]}
vars: {"a":0.01}

    arrow re #3d3833 from.x -1.4 from.y 0 to.x 1.4 to.y 0 hidden true
    label l_re "real" below-right attach_to re hidden true
    arrow im #3d3833 from.x 0 from.y -1.25 to.x 0 to.y 1.25 hidden true
    label l_im "imaginary" above-right attach_to im hidden true
    group ax members ["re","l_re","im","l_im"] hidden true
    dot one #2f6b8f at.x 1 at.y 0 hidden true
    label l_one "1" below-right #2f6b8f attach_to one hidden true
    dot ipt #2f6b8f at.x 0 at.y 1 hidden true
    label l_i "i" above-left #2f6b8f attach_to ipt hidden true
    dot minus #2f6b8f at.x -1 at.y 0 hidden true
    label l_minus "−1" below-left #2f6b8f attach_to minus hidden true
    curve circle dashed #8f887c x_expr cos(t) y_expr sin(t) t_from 0 t_to 6.2832 hidden true
    curve arc #b5482e x_expr 0.28*cos(t) y_expr 0.28*sin(t) t_from 0 t_to 0.01 bind.t_to a hidden true
    dot p #b5482e at.x 1 at.y 0 bind {"at.x":"cos(a)","at.y":"sin(a)"} hidden true
    arrow rad #b5482e from.x 0 from.y 0 to.ref p hidden true
    math eq tex e^{i\pi}=-1 size 34 colors {"\\theta":"#b5482e"} x 780 y 600 hidden true
    text readout "θ = {a:2}" #b5482e x 780 y 500 font_size 28 hidden true
    portrait portrait_richard_feynman x 880 y 250 width 160 of "Richard Feynman" cameo true hidden true
    dot q #8f887c at.x 1 at.y 0 bind {"at.x":"cos(a)"} hidden true
    arrow cosl dashed #2f6b8f from.x 0 from.y 0 to.ref q hidden true
    arrow sinl q -> p dashed #2f6b8f hidden true
    label l_cos "cos θ" below #2f6b8f attach_to cosl hidden true
    label l_sin "sin θ" right #2f6b8f attach_to sinl hidden true

    card title "Euler's identity, as half a turn"

Why does e to the i pi equal minus one? It sounds like nonsense, but it is a picture: half a turn around a circle.
    draw ax eq

It is often called the most beautiful equation in mathematics. To see why it is true, start with the number one.
    draw one l_one

Multiplying by i turns a number a quarter turn: one becomes i, straight up.
    draw ipt l_i

Turn again and you reach minus one — which is all that i squared equals minus one says.
    draw minus l_minus

Between those quarter turns lie all the other angles, on a circle of radius one.
    draw circle

So e to the i theta means: turn from one by an angle theta.
    draw rad p arc readout

    group legs members ["q","cosl","sinl","l_cos","l_sin"]

Turn a little, and the point lands cosine theta across and sine theta up.
    animate a 0.9 duration 2.5

That is Euler's formula: e to the i theta is cosine theta plus i sine theta.
    morph eq tex e^{i\theta}=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta duration 1.5

    erase legs

Now turn by pi radians — a hundred and eighty degrees, half a turn.
    animate a 3.1416 duration 4

    erase readout

    text readout_pi "θ = π" #b5482e x 780 y 500 font_size 28

Half a turn from one lands exactly on minus one.
    highlight minus

So e to the i pi is minus one: not a coincidence of numbers, but a half turn written as a power.
    morph eq tex e^{i\pi}=-1 duration 1.5

Move the one across, and e, i, pi, one and zero share a single line.
    morph eq tex e^{i\pi}+1=0 duration 1.5

At fourteen, Richard Feynman wrote in his notebook that this was the most remarkable formula in mathematics.
    draw portrait_richard_feynman

    erase portrait_richard_feynman

    quiz "Where does e to the 2iπ land?"
        right: At 1: two pi is a full turn, which brings you back to where you started.
        * −1
        + 1
        * 0

## Thumbnail
role: thumbnail
page: {"valign": "none"}
    math eq tex "e^{i\\pi} = -1" x 500 y 560 size 130
    path turn smooth thick #c0392b points [[760,300],[690,400],[500,440],[310,400],[240,300]]
    text t1 "half a turn" x 500 y 330 font_size 64 #c0392b
    thumb w1 "Nonsense? It's half a turn" kind band
    thumb w2 kind star

